diagnosis_text:
A full diagnostic pass — reachability probe, L2/L3 infrastructure sweep, OSPF control-plane snapshot, traffic-control snapshot, service and pressure sweeps, plus direct inspection of carrier counters and FRR daemon logs on all three core routers — found no evidence of any fault.

**Current state:** All OSPF adjacencies between router_core_1, router_core_2, and router_core_3 are in Full state. Every FRR daemon (ospfd, zebra) is healthy on all six routing devices. Each router holds a complete 10-route OSPF table. All client hosts reach all web servers at 0% loss with sub-2 ms RTT. DNS and HTTP services answer correctly.

**Mechanism check (flapping):** Carrier-change counters on every backbone interface (eth0/eth1/eth2) of all three core routers read exactly 2, which is the Kathara startup baseline (one carrier-down + one carrier-up at container launch). No post-boot carrier transitions have occurred. The FRR OSPF log contains only the initial configuration-load and DB-exchange negotiation entries from startup; there are no adjacency-Down, ExStart, or back-to-Full transitions. `dmesg` on each core router returns zero carrier-related messages. No flap script or stress-tool process is running on any device.

**Root cause:** The NOC report of "OSPF core adjacency flapping" is unconfirmed. There is no fault, no historical trace, and no mechanism that could explain intermittent adjacency drops. The network is operating normally.

**Fix:** No corrective action is required. The reported instability does not correspond to any observable event in the lab; the ticket can be closed as a false alarm or further investigated via out-of-band NOC monitoring records.
=== END ===